===Etymology 3===
[[Image:Ruta chalepensis11.jpg|120px|thumb|Rue (plant)]]
From {{inh|en|enm|rue}}, from {{der|en|xno|ruwe}}, {{der|en|fro|rue}}, from {{der|en|la|rūta}}, from {{der|en|grc|ῥυτή}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|plant}} Any of various [[perennial]] [[shrubs]] of the genus ''[[Ruta]]'', especially the herb {{taxfmt|Ruta graveolens|species}} ([[common rue]]), formerly used in medicines.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|III|2|text=But th'aged Nourse, her calling to her bowre, / Had gathered '''Rew''', and Savine, and the flowre / Of Camphora, and Calamint, and Dill [...].}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|4|5|text={{w|Ophelia}}:<br>There’s fennel for you, and columbines: there’s '''rue''' for you; and here’s some for me: we may call it herb-grace o' Sundays: O you must wear your '''rue''' with a difference.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1940|author=Rosetta E. Clarkson|title=Green Enchantments: The Magic Spell of Gardens |publisher=The Macmillan Company|page=253|text=The life of one plant would be affected by another. '''Rue''' was definitely hostile to basil, rosemary to hyssop, but coriander, dill and chervil lived on the friendliest of terms[.]}}

